    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We start by setting up an inequality to represent the given condition. Let x be the integer we are looking for. Then, we have:\n\nx^2 < 2x\n\nTo solve this inequality, we can rearrange it as follows:\n\nx^2 - 2x < 0\n\nNow, we can factor out an x from the left side:\n\nx(x - 2) < 0\n\nThis inequality is satisfied when x and x - 2 have opposite signs. We can analyze the sign of x(x - 2) by considering the values of x in the following intervals:\n\n1. x < 0\n2. 0 < x < 2\n3. x > 2\n\nFor x < 0, both x and x - 2 are negative, so their product is positive.\n\nFor 0 < x < 2, x is positive and x - 2 is negative, so their product is negative.\n\nFor x > 2, both x and x - 2 are positive, so their product is positive.\n\nTherefore, the inequality x(x - 2) < 0 is satisfied when 0 < x < 2. The only integer in this interval is x = 1.\n\nSo, the only integer whose square is less than its double is \\boxed{1}.",
